elling device that the anime has  access to one of the things I really  love about the promised Neverland is how  visually distinct it is in regards to  shock composition and editing editing in  particular frequently takes advantage of  jarring cuts to elicit truly unnerving  responses and one of the best examples I  can think of is in episode 2 which went  from a shot of Emma to a shot of Norman  and when he peeked around the corner  there was an immediate cut to mother  standing with Emma and legitimately jump  scared the crap out of me it was  fantastic and there were a plethora of  other well employed visual techniques  that really added an air of tension and  just plain weirdness kind of like the  tendency to have a close-up shot on a  character's neck while they were  speaking really highlighting their  number as well as the fact that they are  more of a product than they are people  in this world plus there were other  great classic tension building shots  like all the first person perspective  walking through hallway moments each and  every one of them had me convinced the  jumps  was imminent because that's how  effectively they filled the atmosphere  with creepy pressure although I will say  that I'm glad that the series did not  try to sustain that feeling of horror  throughout and instead opted to explore  tension in its various forms and you  know when I think about it I'm not  really sure what genre I would throw the  promise Neverland into because over its  12-episode run it seemed to cover at  least four different genres to me the  first of which was that horror thriller  aspect of the early episodes but then  that kind of subsided and it turned into  more of a mind game style series  somewhat akin to Death Note but then it  radically shifted again into a more  depressing tale of fate within the  system and then finally it turned into a  good old-fashioned to break out story  and I have nothing but praise for how  the series shifted so seamlessly through  these various ideas especially since the  entire thing is condensed into a very  simple single location it was a superb  show of artistry from the production  team the actors and I'm assuming the  original source material as well in the  manga as for the characters despite the  fact that we have an orphanage full of  what thirty eight children and two  adults at most at any given time primary  focus was only given to a handful of  individuals which i think is a great  choice by the end of these twelve  episodes no fundamental character felt  undercooked and they did chant a solid  amount of development plus the fact that  we really didn't expand on the other  orphans at all with a few exceptions of  course made the eventual climax of the  series all the better when we saw  flashbacks of them all training for the  escape which had been taking place in  the background while our focus was  elsewhere it was really satisfying and  of course my favorite character was  definitely Emma she's instantly likable  because of a kind and idealistic nature  but after the first couple of episodes I  do remember being really worried that  she would become the relentless optimist  who would continue to insist on having  her ideals met and I was pleasantly  surprised to throughout the run Emmit  developed and matured significantly to  the point where she amended her original  idea of having all of the kids escape  but without losing any of her core  values as she bound to return for those  they had left behind it was also really  impressive that she was so mentally  capable of dealing with the situations  that's presented to her i think emma is  one of those characters who at first  glance is easy to write off as a bit of  an airhead a lovely person but not the  kind of character who you know handles  the thinking and I love just how wrong  that thought of mine ended up being and  yes I know it's established from the  very beginning that she's one of the  smartest kids in the facility but that  wasn't how she generally presented  herself outside of a classroom which  made her moments of exceptional thinking  all the more  in regards to the idea of thinking  though I will say that there were times  within the promised Neverland where it  really pushed the idea of how  intelligent these kids are to the very  extreme given that they were all under  12 years old kind of complexity coming  from them was absolutely insane  especially when it came to things like  gray being able to build a device that  disables their trackers that in  particular was a bit hard to buy because  I know that these kids gifted but  they've lived on what is more or less  farmland actually quite literally  farmland I guess with no access to any  form of advanced technology outside of  what Ray brought in as his prizes but  even with that there would be assumedly  no reasonable source of knowledge or  tools to put such a thing together and I  guess it's not series breaking and I  know that Ray does have a particularly  godlike memory but it does require that  extra suspension of disbelief and of  course there's also fill'd it was a four  year old boy and quite possibly the most  naturally intelligent character within  the entire series it's completely absurd  but phil is just so damn adorable that  he gets away with it  but all in all I do have to give more  high praise to how well the kids were  written as characters it is such a  difficult thing to write kids for a  teenage or even worse an adult audience  because generally you have two choices  you can either make them really smart  thus garnering the hatred of adults who  see the child as an inconceivable genius  type you know the Wesley Crusher effect  oh you write them as realistic kids in  which case adults take little to no  interest in them because they have very  little depth they're always predictable  and they won't generally be capable of  contributing anything major to the story  due to their age and an experience the  promise Neverland has somehow managed to  avoid that issue even though the kids in  the show embodied both of those  theoretical problems at some points  being absurdly smart and at others being  incredibly simple and childlike yes I'm  looking at you  dawn however I don't think either of  those crossed into the realm of your  typical annoying child characters they  were all very nicely rounded and  compelling figures who I really got  behind for the duration of the series  there is one thing that did kind of  annoy me about syriza and it's how the  character of Norman is eventually dealt  with so here's the deal right there is  absolutely no way this kid is dead i  100% refuse to believe that his final  moments were being ushered into a  brightly lit room and being asked to  wait I would bet large quantities of  currency that he is live and will appear  again later down the line now this in  and of itself isn't so much of a problem  to me but rather how it impacted  juxtaposed moments for example the  entire episode leading up until the  point was simply heartbreaking with  Norman and the rest of the kids coming  to terms with the fact that there was  nothing they could do and that he was  going to be killed  watching Norman succumb to terror in the  bathroom but eventually bravely meeting  his end for the sake of helping the  other kids is such a beautiful and  tragic idea but what it turned into has  the potential to undercut that to me if  what I suspect is true and you know the  99.9% chance of him being alive turns  out to be accurate and I think it takes  away from the drama and focus put on his  brave decision to sacrifice himself it's  something that I really hate in all  forms of media making the idea of the  death of a character into a substantial  plot point only to bring them back later  and thus negate all of the drama you  took the time to create using their  quote-unquote death it's what really  annoys me about western comics as well  as sequels to movies in general who  boldly kill a character only to bring  them back for the sequel somehow because  they were popular and just to examine  the flipside for a second if Norman is  actually dead then this is a pathetic  final moment from a storytelling  perspective and it was a huge misstep  which I refused to believe because up  until this point everything felt so  sturdy to me also going further there  were some more beautiful moments during  the escape  Norman's present somewhat appeared as if  it was his final legacy being enacted  and that once again will be undercut if  it turns out that he was alive all along  but that is really the only thing that  came close to bothering me about the  promise Neverland otherwise it was a  ride that I was completely on board with  and I'm very keen to see more in the  future and may there is still so much  they haven't even touched on like the  great characters of Mother Isabella and  sister Crone with sister Crone in  particular she was an exceptionally  creepy presence for the majority of her  time in the series I think her voice  actor did a wonderful job of bringing  her to life as did the animators who  gave some nice delicate attention to her  eccentricities and I was very pleasantly  surprised when I actually came to feel  for her as a character right before she  was killed and it's not because of  anything she did she was a generally  terrible human being but it has  everything to do with her doll because  you know at first it seemed like sister  Crona had that deranged mother fantasy  which to be fair she kind of did but it  hit me so hard when we were showing that  that doll was actually her toy from when  she was raised to stock on one of the  human farms that incredibly simple  connection activated all of the fields  within me and I was genuinely sad when  she met her demise I can't really say I  ended up feeling the same way about  Isabella though as a character she fall  at the same sort of trajectory as  sister Kron starting out as a horrific  ly creepy presence and coming to be  somewhat redeemed and understood by the  end she was also a victim of the ways of  this world however she didn't have that  moment of sharply connecting to me like  sister Brian and I still very much  dislike Isabella but she know after  facilitating the murder of many many  children I think is quite fair she was  still the great character though and a  very imposing presence now there is a  further step here within the system  because we have sisters mothers and even  and grandmother who was hands down the  number one Creepo in the promise  Neverland and what I'm really hoping is  that if she does have an eventual arc it  doesn't follow the story of trying to  generate any sort of sympathy whatsoever  at the moment to me grandma is pretty  much the epitome of evil in the series  much more so than the demons could ever  be and I'd very much like her to remain  that way speaking of the demons though  I'm not entirely sure how to feel about  them part of me wishes that we hadn't  seen so much of them and they were kept  as a more mystified existence but I have  to admit that that would only work for  the story to a certain extent after  knowing how this anime series concluded  I do understand why it was a good idea  to introduce their physical forms very  clearly on-screen at an early stage  because I imagine that we're about to go  out into a world we're seeing them is  fairly commonplace and it might be a bit  jarring to have weird-looking demons be  a part of the series all of a sudden by  the way some real Tokyo when I first saw  the end of episode 1 I thought that  there were aliens I didn't realize there  were demons until one of the characters  actually said the word demon and then I  was like oh I am like I guess they could  also be demons but in conclusion the  romo stimulant was a very nice surprise  I'm glad I got to watch it all at once  because I'm honestly not sure that it  would have been as good an experience  waiting weakly but highly recommended to  all even those of you who haven't seen  it and are well and truly spoiled if  you've made it this far into the video  but that pretty much does it for my  thoughts on the promise Neverland if you  enjoyed this video and the content this  channel produced in general then please  do consider donating to the new world -  via patreon because the support of all  the amazing people is what continues to  make this channel possible and if  patreon isn't quite your style then  please do leave this video a like share  or subscribe because it also helps  support the channel an incredible amount  and if you'd like to join the fun at any  time then please do head over to main  discord server where a wide array of  shenanigan ray takes place on a daily  basis and finally please do comment with  your thoughts on the promised neverland  this has been the new world review and  I'll see you next time

